---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ComparisonTable from "../components/ComparisonTable.astro";
import { SITE } from "../site";

const L = SITE.links;

const boundary = [
  {
    left: "Source for labs, tools, apps and the sites I build",
    right: "Credentials, tokens, keys and any other secrets",
  },
  {
    left: "Brand tokens, shared styles, policies and the glossary",
    right: "Account and binding identifiers, internal hostnames and private URLs",
  },
  {
    left: "API contracts, schemas and structured data formats",
    right: "The exact rules of access policies",
  },
  {
    left: "Planning boards for public projects",
    right: "Private repositories, and services that are restricted by design",
  },
];

const principles = [
  {
    title: "Build in public where it adds value",
    tone: "teal",
    text: "Sharing work helps when it lets someone learn from it, check it, reuse an idea or point out a mistake. I publish with that test in mind rather than publishing everything by default.",
  },
  {
    title: "Keep infrastructure secure and private",
    tone: "violet",
    text: "Public means curated. The code and the thinking can be open without exposing how the estate is operated or protected. That line is drawn on purpose, and documented.",
  },
  {
    title: "Show maturity honestly",
    tone: "amber",
    text: "Experiments, finished tools and full applications are kept apart so you can tell what you are looking at. Labs may be rough; apps are expected to be dependable; claims I have not verified are marked as such.",
  },
  {
    title: "Prefer portable, open formats",
    tone: "sky",
    text: "Structured data, documented schemas, open standards and exportable data make work easier to reuse, verify and migrate, and avoid depending on opaque platforms.",
  },
  {
    title: "Write decisions down",
    tone: "coral",
    text: "A repository without its reasoning is only half shared. I record architecture decisions and keep documentation alongside the code, especially where a system will be reused.",
  },
  {
    title: "Make it accessible and understandable",
    tone: "teal",
    text: "Semantic HTML, plain language, clear structure and performance are requirements, not extras. Open work should be readable by people as well as by machines.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Open source"
  description="How Ryan Laird approaches open source: what is published, what stays private, and the principles behind each decision."
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Dev</a> / Open source</nav>

    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="sl">Open source</p>
      <h1>How I approach open source</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        I build in public where it adds value, and keep infrastructure secure and private. This page explains
        what that means in practice: what I publish, what I do not, and why.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/repos/">Browse the repositories</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/boards/">See the planning boards</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="principles-title">
      <p class="tlbl">Principles</p>
      <h2 id="principles-title">Six working rules</h2>
      <div class="route-grid">
        {principles.map((p) => (
          <article class="route" data-tone={p.tone}>
            <h3>{p.title}</h3>
            <p>{p.text}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="public-title">
      <p class="tlbl">What I publish</p>
      <h2 id="public-title">The work, and the thinking around it</h2>
      <p>
        Most of what you will find on GitHub falls into a few groups, each separated so its maturity is clear:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Labs</strong>: experiments and prototypes exploring ideas across space and science, where it is fine for things to be unfinished.</li>
        <li><strong>Tools and apps</strong>: focused utilities and calculators, and more complete applications, each with a shared catalogue.</li>
        <li><strong>Sites and data</strong>: the personal site and CV, an API for structured portfolio data, and a service-status dashboard.</li>
        <li><strong>Foundations</strong>: shared brand tokens and guidelines, version-controlled policies, and a glossary of terms used across the work.</li>
        <li><strong>Research tooling</strong>: tools for collecting and organising research feeds.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The <a href="/repos/">repositories page</a> is generated from GitHub at build time, so it reflects
        what is actually public rather than a hand-maintained list. Planning is public too, through the
        <a href="/boards/">project boards</a>, but only for boards that are public.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="private-title">
      <p class="tlbl">Where the line is</p>
      <h2 id="private-title">Public by design, private by design</h2>
      <p>
        Openness and security are not opposites if the boundary is explicit. I keep the following rules for
        anything I publish, including documentation:
      </p>
      <ComparisonTable
        leftTitle="Published"
        rightTitle="Kept private"
        rows={boundary}
        caption="What I publish compared with what I keep private"
      />
      <p>
        Automated tooling follows the same rule. The site build uses public data wherever it can, falls back
        to a saved snapshot if GitHub cannot be reached, and ignores any private board, even if one is
        present in the data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="reuse-title">
      <p class="tlbl">Reuse and contribution</p>
      <h2 id="reuse-title">Using my work, and working with me</h2>
      <p>
        Public code is there to read, learn from and discuss. Reuse is governed by the licence in each
        repository: where a repository includes a licence file, that is what applies, and where it does not,
        please assume that all rights are reserved and ask me before reusing it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Spotted a mistake or have an idea? Open an issue on the relevant repository. For anything larger, such
        as a collaboration, consulting or a technical project, the best starting point is
        <a href={L.contact} rel="noopener">my main site</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="docs-title">
      <p class="tlbl">Documentation</p>
      <h2 id="docs-title">Where the reasoning lives</h2>
      <p>
        Architecture notes, decisions, runbooks and reference material are documented separately from this
        site, so the code stays light and the explanation stays maintained in one place.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn" href={L.docs} rel="noopener">Read the documentation ↗</a>
        <a class="btn" href={L.github} rel="me noopener">GitHub profile ↗</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/stack/">The stack I use</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
